    SerializationNodes: []
  _udonSharpBackingUdonBehaviour: {fileID: 1019700296}
  titles:
  - The Apollo 11 broadcast
  - The first quasar
  - From black holes to Wi-Fi
  - Australian radio telescopes
  - The birth of radio astronomy
  questions:
  - Place the moments from the Apollo 11 moonwalk broadcast in the order they occurred
    .
  - Place the steps in the discovery of the first quasar, 3C 273, in the order they
    occurred.
  - Place these in order of how a search for exploding black holes end up giving
    us Wi-Fi?
  - Put these Australian radio astronomy milestones in the order in which they happened.
  - Place these moments from the start of radio astronomy in Sydney in the order
    they happened.
  sphereLabels:
  - Fire at Tidbinbilla
  - Camera on
  - One small step
  - Parkes takes over
  - The Moon passes in front
  - A spectrum at Palomar
  - The redshift clicks
  - Published in Nature
  - Black holes might explode
  - The search
  - The patent
  - The first chip
  - Parkes opens
  - The Compact Array opens
  - The Murchison observatory opens
  - SKA-Low construction starts
  - Noise at sunrise
  - Sunspots and radio noise
  - Sunrise on the cliff
  - A big new idea
  sphereInfoTexts:
  - Because of a fire at the Tidbinbilla tracking station there was a forced change
    of plan to swap with Honeysuckle Creek, which will now follow the lunar module
    during the moonwalk.
  - "Buzz Aldrin turns on the lunar module\u2019s TV camera while the Parkes radio
    telescope battles strong winds, outside of its safety limits."
  - When Neil Armstrong steps onto the Moon. The pictures come through Honeysuckle
    Creek near Canberra which all of the world see.
  - About eight and a half minutes into the broadcast, the main detector at Parkes
    locks on and the NASA one switches to the Parkes pictures for the rest of the
    moonwalk.
  - At Parkes astronomers time the exact moments a radio source called 3C 273 disappears
    behind the Moon and then reappears, pinning down its exact position.
  - Maarten Schmidt takes a spectrum of a starlike object near that position at the
    Palomar 200 inch telescope.
  - Schmidt realises the strange lines  shifted far to the red are ordinary hydrogen.
    The object is enormously far away.
  - Nature publishes the Parkes position and Schmidt's redshift together in the same
    issue.
  - Stephen Hawking made a prediction that small black holes could slowly evaporate
    and give off radio signals as they vanish.
  - When John O'Sullivan and colleagues search for these faint smeared signals, they
    could not find the black holes but they got very good at cleaning up messy radio
    data.
  - CSIRO files an Australian patent on fast reliable wireless networking based on
    ideas developed through radio astronomy.
  - The world's first chip for the 802.11a Wi-Fi standard was demonstrated by Radiata,
    which is a CSIRO and Macquarie University spin-off .
  - A 64 metre dish near Parkes is officially opened by the Governor General. which
    was named Murriyang in 2020 by Wiradjuri elders.
  - Prime Minister Bob Hawke officially opened six 22 metre dishes at Narrabri.
  - A remote radio quiet observatory which is home to ASKAP formally opened on Wajarri
    Yamaji Country in Western Australia.
  - Construction on SKA-Low begins, which is part of the world's largest radio telescope
    project, located at Inyarrimanha Ilgari Bundara in Western Australia.
  - Strange radio noise was noticed by radar operators on Norfolk Island each time
    the Sun rises.
  - Air force radar was used at Collaroy, by Joe Pawsey, Ruby Payne Scott and Lindsay
    McCready to find out that the Sun's radio noise rises and falls with sunspot
    activity.
  - In early 1946, Ruby Payne Scott uses an Army radar on the cliffs at Dover Heights
    to make what were probably the first interferometer observations in radio astronomy.
  - The idea behind radio arrays like ASKAP was first described as aperture synthesis
    by Payne Scott and Pawsey.
  hint1:
  - One of these happened a few days before the moonwalk.
  - You need to know where something is before you can point a telescope at it.
  - The theory usually comes before anyone goes looking for it.
  - Parkes was used for the Apollo 11 broadcast so it must have been around by 1969.
  - Someone had to notice something odd before anyone set out to study it.
  hint2:
  - Parkes's main detector could only used once the Moon had risen high enough.
  - A discovery needs to be made before the scientists can publish it.
  - You can not build a product before the idea has been worked out and is protected.
  - The Compact Array was opened in 1988 which is Australia's 200th anniversary.
  - The big idea came after the cliff top experiments showed what was possible.
  correctText:
  - That is right! The Tidbinbilla fire changed the plan, Aldrin turned on the camera,
    Honeysuckle Creek carried the first step, and Parkes took over about eight and
    a half minutes in. The film The Dish shows Parkes broadcasting the first step,
    but that part is not quite true.
  - That is Correct! Parkes timed the Moon passing in front of 3C 273 in 1962, Schmidt
    took the spectrum, worked out the redshift on 5 February 1963, and the papers
    came out together that March.
  - Right! Hawking's 1974 idea led O'Sullivan's team to search for exploding black
    holes. They never found them, but their methods led to CSIRO's 1992 Australian
    patent on wireless networking and Radiata's first 802.11a chip.
  - Correct! Parkes opened in 1961, the Compact Array in 1988, the Murchison observatory
    in 2012, and SKA-Low construction started in December 2022.
  - Correct! Radar operators noticed noise at sunrise in early 1945, Pawsey's team
    linked it to sunspots later that year, Payne-Scott made the cliff top interferometer
    observations in early 1946, and by mid 1946 she and Pawsey had described aperture
    synthesis.
  revealText:
  - 'The order: the fire at Tidbinbilla, Aldrin switching on the camera, the first
    step seen through Honeysuckle Creek, then Parkes taking over about eight and
    a half minutes in.'
  - 'The order: the Parkes occultation in 1962, Schmidt''s spectrum at Palomar, the
    redshift on 5 February 1963, then the Nature papers in March 1963.'
  - 'The order: Hawking''s prediction in 1974, O''Sullivan''s search for exploding
    black holes, CSIRO''s Australian patent in 1992, then Radiata''s first 802.11a
    chip.'
  - 'The order: Parkes in 1961, the Compact Array in 1988, the Murchison observatory
    in 2012, then SKA-Low construction in 2022.'
  - 'The order: noise at sunrise on Norfolk Island in 1945, the Collaroy sunspot
    work later in 1945, the Dover Heights observations in early 1946, then aperture
    synthesis in mid 1946.'
